        "body": "South Carolina's tracked data center capacity stands at 2 MW across 14 listed facilities, a figure so low it reflects the limits of available disclosure rather than the state's actual infrastructure weight.\n\nThe facility list is dominated by two operators. DartPoints runs colocation sites in four cities, Columbia, Charleston, Greenville, and Spartanburg, providing regional network presence across the state. Google accounts for nine entries in Berkeley County, with coordinates clustered within a very small area, indicating a single campus recorded at multiple points rather than nine independent buildings. Only one facility, DartPoints Columbia, carries a MW figure, and that single 2 MW value is the entirety of the state total. The remaining thirteen facilities have no power data attached, so the 2 MW number should be read as a floor, not a ceiling.\n\nFor AI infrastructure purposes, the data does not support conclusions about compute density or grid load in South Carolina. The Google Berkeley County campus is the kind of large-scale facility that typically draws hundreds of megawatts and anchors regional grid planning, but no capacity figure for it appears here. The DC BLOX Myrtle Beach entry is identified as a cable landing station, which matters for data transit rather than local compute. What the data does confirm is that South Carolina has colocation presence across multiple metros and at least one hyperscale operator footprint, but the power record is too incomplete to place the state in any meaningful ranking of AI-relevant grid demand.",
